#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256897 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "82ccfef3-569a-5ddf-9a69-cae323a0a312" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Possible Roots of a Polynomial Using the Rational Roots Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f1100322-f9f9-5124-a50e-27f9ee343c35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256899}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f1100322-f9f9-5124-a50e-27f9ee343c35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256899} :answer-field/id #uuid "99d9f829-2ab1-507e-a54e-e1c936f87e15" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256898}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9e4c35d0-80ea-555f-a42d-fa766d0cba07" :question/math-academy-id "q-112867" :question/problem "According to the rational roots theorem, What could be a root of the polynomial $f(x)=8{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+10?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $10,$ and the leading coefficient is $8.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }10}{\\text{factor of }8}.\n$$\n\nOf the given answer choices, only $-\\frac{5}{4}=\\frac{-5}{4}$ takes the above form since the numerator $(-5)$ is a factor of $10$ and the denominator $(4)$ is a factor of $8.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256900 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "82ccfef3-569a-5ddf-9a69-cae323a0a312" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Possible Roots of a Polynomial Using the Rational Roots Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "51b3db7c-057e-5c4b-934f-a7beba01180a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256902}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "51b3db7c-057e-5c4b-934f-a7beba01180a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256902} :answer-field/id #uuid "2935c1f0-080f-504b-bd7e-60952c1de321" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256901}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "284a91b3-0aac-5ff1-be86-b1f45987e7b5" :question/math-academy-id "q-112871" :question/problem "According to the rational roots theorem, What could be a root of the polynomial $f(x)=4{x}^{7}-12{x}^{4}+5{x}^{2}-3x+10?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $10,$ and the leading coefficient is $4.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }10}{\\text{factor of }4}.\n$$\n\nOf the given answer choices, only $\\frac{5}{4}$ takes the above form since the numerator $(5)$ is a factor of $10$ and the denominator $(4)$ is a factor of $4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261882 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a75b47ec-8d5a-545e-b915-daca92320322" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Non-Roots of a Polynomial Using the Rational Roots Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30905c28-cb3e-546b-9dc8-5fce5d94c32e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261884}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "30905c28-cb3e-546b-9dc8-5fce5d94c32e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261884} :answer-field/id #uuid "f1aef993-e83d-5c8c-b93e-e98f5353e4cc" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261883}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a045cc6b-4cfa-5c4d-a695-16b0b2a1fa9a" :question/math-academy-id "q-83815" :question/problem "According to the rational roots theorem, What **cannot** be a root of the polynomial $f(x)=2{x}^{3}+5{x}^{2}+x-3?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $-3$ and the leading coefficient is $2.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }-3}{\\text{factor of }2}.\n$$\n\nOf the given answer choices, only $2$ does not take the above form. The value $2$ can be interpreted as a fraction $\\frac{2}{1},$ and the numerator $(2)$ is not a factor of $-3.$"}]}
